{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cd4vMYJbHAJyw2SRWz8bu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four acceptance scenarios pass — 4 scenarios, 26 steps, zero failures. (The garbled Korean in the output is just the Windows console's code page mangling UTF-8; the test results themselves are clean.)\n\n**What I built** — five files in `src/main/java/com/thinking/ticket/`, nothing touched in `provided/`, `src/test/`, or `pom.xml`:\n\n- `TicketService.java` — the contracted entry point, with the exact constructor and `reserveTicket` signature from `contract.md`.\n- `Ticket.java` — a package-private domain object that holds the reservation rule.\n- The three exceptions (`UserNotFoundException`, `TicketAlreadyReservedException`, `PaymentFailedException`), each extending `RuntimeException`.\n\n**Two decisions worth flagging:**\n\nThe order of operations in `reserveTicket` is forced by the rules rather than arbitrary. Both rejection reasons (unregistered member, already-reserved ticket) are checked *before* `payments.charge` is called, because rules 1 and 2 say no charge may occur. The reservation is only committed after the charge is approved, because rule 3 says a declined charge leaves the ticket unclaimed.\n\n`Ticket` copies values out of `TicketRecord` instead of holding a reference to it, and this is load-bearing rather than stylistic. `TicketStore` is an in-memory simulator, so `findById` hands back the *same live object* the test seeded. Had `Ticket` wrapped that record and mutated it directly, a later payment failure would have left `reserved = true` visible in the store even though nothing was ever saved — silently failing the declined-payment scenario. Copying means changes only escape through `toRecord()` at save time, so an aborted reservation leaves the store untouched by construction.\n\nOne thing deliberately left alone: `spec.md` says the behavior for a ticket that isn't in the store is out of scope, so I didn't invent a rule for it. `Ticket.from(null)` will throw an NPE there. If you'd rather it surface as a named exception, that's a small addition — but it would mean deciding a question the spec explicitly deferred."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2431,"cache_read_input_tokens":43051,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2431},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0b3940c-305c-4aa2-b53e-bc30372a96e0","uuid":"005379c8-cede-49e9-bbdb-938b000b4de1","timestamp":"2026-07-16T02:08:10.096Z","request_id":"req_011Cd4vMXAdrsckEheWfVj4R"}
